title: One value type per field name
tags: [[Data model]] [[Key invariants]]
kind: reference
summary: A field name has one value type in the whole repository, fixed by its first write; nothing coexists with it.

A field name has a ''single value type'' across the whole repository: every `tag`
row is a `string`, every `author` row a `ref`. `nothing` always coexists with it —
it is absence, not a type: a field is its type where it applies and `nothing`
where it does not. The multi-map is unaffected: many rows of one name, all of the
one type.

The type is fixed by the first write of a value that is not `nothing`; a schema may
also declare it ([[Schema]]). A later write of another type is refused with `400`:

```
field 'rating' has value type 'int'; cannot write a 'string' value (use retype to change the field's type)
```

The store counts, per field name, the rows of each type, so the check reads one key
(cached for the rest of the revision). When a name's last value is removed, its type
is free again. To change the type of a field that has values, retype it
([[Changing a field's type]]).

The rule keeps the rest of the model simple: a comparison is always between two
values of one type, and a sort needs no precedence between types. `GET /fields` and
`mf field list` list each name once, with its type.
